# Flutter/Dart build output and tool state. Everything here is regenerated by
# `flutter pub get` / `flutter build`, and the lock file is deliberately NOT
# ignored -- an app whose dependency versions drift between machines is an app
# that builds on one laptop and not the other.
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
.packages
build/
*.iml

# Signing. A keystore in the repository is a signing key everybody has, and a
# build signed with a new key is one no installed phone will accept as an
# upgrade -- there is no way back from that except uninstalling every handset
# by hand.
*.jks
*.keystore
key.properties

# Local overrides
.env

# Platform folders that are not this app's targets. `flutter create .` writes
# all six; android/ and ios/ are tracked because the handsets in the shops run
# those two, and these four are regenerated by that same command on any
# machine that ever needs them -- the sibling Nexus mobile app tracks the same
# two and no more. Ignored rather than deleted: deleting them means a `flutter
# create .` on the next machine puts them straight back into `git status`.
/linux/
/macos/
/web/
/windows/

# The IDE's own view of the project -- window layout, run configurations, the
# path to one person's Dart SDK.
.idea/
